   "source": [
    "## Clean data using Interpolation"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4b40db69-531d-4c2f-8221-1291af2c2af4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Explanation as to why these methods are a bit restrictive in cases when you are dealing with the values such as temperature\n",
    "Methods in question\n",
    "- Another way of getting bettwe estimate is to carry forward the temperature of one row previous of the row where 0 is present.\n",
    "- Another way of getting bettwe estimate is to carry forward the temperature of one row next of the row where 0 is present.\n",
    "<br>\n",
    "Suppose there on the day 1 we have a temperature -2.9 degrees then on day 2 the df has a NaN value and on day 3 the df has 14 degree temperature\n",
    "<br>\n",
    "Case 1 : Another way of getting bettwe estimate is to carry forward the temperature of one row previous of the row where 0 is present. <br>\n",
    "day 1 --> -2.9 <br>\n",
    "day 2 --> -2.9 <br>\n",
    "day 3 --> 14 <br>\n",
    "<br>\n",
    "Case 2 : Another way of getting bettwe estimate is to carry forward the temperature of one row next of the row where 0 is present. <br>\n",
    "day 1 --> -2.9 <br>\n",
    "day 2 --> 14 <br>\n",
    "day 3 --> 14 <br>\n",
    "\n",
    "In both the cases the temperature between days 1 to 3 doesn't rise gradually it raises abruptly and then the two consecutive days have the same temperature in real life that's not how it works the temperature rises normally and gradually. So how can we deal with the NaN values in the temperature column or in the dataFrame as a whole?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c1492ae4-e217-44dc-905b-e797258d950b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### What is interpolation ? \n",
    "```DataFrame.interpolation()``` is a pandas method used to fill in missing values NaN by estimating or guessing what the values should be, based on surrounding data. <br>\n",
    "Here is the link to the docs related to this topic : https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/dev/reference/api/pandas.DataFrame.interpolate.html <br>\n",
    "```DataFrame.interpolate(method='linear', *, axis=0, limit=None, inplace=False, limit_direction=None, limit_area=None, **kwargs)```"
   ]
  },
